00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
the tax laws of this country are a  disgrace to the human race  our tax system could only be described  as un-american  read my lips  taxes they're in every four years  campaign trail issue  and just like most modern day candidates  joe biden  campaigned on big ambitions his  to tax the rich give money to the middle  class and  roll back some parts of president  trump's tax overhaul  we don't need a tax code that rewards  wealth  more than it rewards work he wants to  terminate the tax cuts that we gave you  joe biden has been very clear  he will not raise taxes on anybody who  makes less than four hundred thousand  dollars a year appeal the trump tax cuts  mr vice president i'm speaking biden  isn't proposing to repeal the entire tax  law  but now that he's president-elect the  reality of u.s politics is setting in  if democrats don't have the senate the  tax increases  are almost certainly not happening it's  that simple  democrats have kept their majority in  the house but  in order for them to gain control of  congress they need to win  two more senate seats which will be  decided in two january runoff races in  georgia  still biden would have to get every  single democratic senator on board with  his proposals  and in the end any changes he's able to  make to the tax code  could look pretty different than the  controversial proposals we heard about  on the campaign trail  so just how likely is biden's tax plan  to become a reality  first let's look at how it could affect  your wallet biden campaign when he was  running really drew this  as much of a bright line at four hundred  thousand dollars of income as they could  four hundred thousand four hundred  thousand dollars a year four hundred  thousand dollars  more than ninety eight percent of  households are below four hundred  thousand dollars so  tax rates and deductions and  big changes that would raise your tax  bill largely  don't affect you if you're below 400 000  of income  the details of exactly how much tax  rates might change are still hard to pin  down but we can look generally at how  biden's tax proposals might affect  different households  a low-income household doesn't pay a lot  in income taxes right now  they probably pay a fair amount in  payroll taxes  taxes on low-income families wouldn't go  up  and not much would change for  middle-income households the  tax rates wouldn't change the tax  brackets wouldn't change  the major deductions wouldn't change the  biggest moves in the 2017 tax law don't  expire until the end of 2025 and at this  point i really wouldn't expect congress  to do much about them  in the in the near term biden has  proposed a number of tax credits that  could give money back to low and  middle-income households  he wants to raise the child tax credit  from two thousand dollars to three  thousand dollars a child or  more for young children under the age of  six families can currently get up to  roughly a thousand dollars per child  through the child and dependent care tax  credit  biden has proposed raising that to a  maximum of four thousand dollars a child  and first-time home buyers could get an  advanceable tax credit  of up to fifteen thousand dollars under  biden's proposed first  down payment tax credit but like we said  the biggest proposed changes would  impact income above four hundred  thousand dollars  yeah high income households if you're  making over 400 000  they want to raise your taxes they want  to raise the tax rates on ordinary  income  they want to get rid of the special new  20  deduction for business income again like  these are the the things that would mark  significant tax increases on high income  households  what we don't know is whether they're  going to be able to get any of them in  place  another major part of biden's tax plan  is corporate taxes  while much of trump's signature tax  policy cut taxes on corporations  biden plans to raise them the buying  approach on taxing businesses is to  basically say the 2017 tax law went too  far  biden's proposal would put the corporate  tax rate at 28 percent  plus any state or local income taxes  that's higher than it is now but not as  high as it was during the obama  administration  a 28 baseline corporate tax rate would  still be higher than the average rate  globally  and major industrialized economies like  hong kong  the uk south korea and canada  one of biden's proposals would put a  minimum tax on companies  so that all large corporations pay at  least some federal taxes  essentially getting rid of instances  where companies like amazon  chevron and starbucks can sometimes pay  an effective u.s  tax rate of zero percent or less  according to their financial statements  why that's possible now is complicated  congress offers companies tax breaks for  investing in things like research and  development and capital equipment  taking those incentives away would  require a delicate balancing act  you know this this idea that congress is  trying to like it's in some ways like  sort of trying to do two things at once  it's like provide these incentives but  then also take them away and so that's  that's going to be a  a tricky thing to kind of work through  once they  go from campaign sound bite into details  on that and  corporate taxes don't just affect people  at the very top  if you're making 150 000 and you  have a 401k and there's corporate shares  in there  then clearly to the extent that  corporate taxes go up corporations are  less profitable  they're worthless that affects the value  of your shares  and so so there's a burden on you from  these tax changes  even if you're not directly having to  pay out more money biden's approach is  to try and line up permanent new  spending with tax increases  the university of pennsylvania ran the  numbers and estimated that biden's plan  would raise 3.375 trillion dollars in  new revenue over 10 years  while increasing spending by 5.37  trillion  so the tax hikes wouldn't cover  everything  and they need to think that through as  they write out the details  economists generally agree that  increased taxes lower economic growth  but by exactly how much is up for debate  of course all of this debate over the  tax code is happening in the middle of a  pandemic  and economic recession in the short term  the government is prioritizing putting  money in people's pockets so they can  make ends meet  that means tax cuts for low and  middle-income americans  could be easier to enact quickly because  they can be tacked onto coronavirus  stimulus  measures but raising corporate and high  income taxes  which is part of biden's long-term plan  at a time when  so many businesses are struggling could  be tricky regardless of who holds power  in the senate in terms of tax increases  it depends on really whether democrats  have the senate  and if they do they're going to face an  interesting choice which is  do they make those tax increases  effective  january of 2021 or do they kind of say  all right well we know the economy is a  little bit weak now and we'll sort of  have them kick in in 2022 or later and  that's something they haven't really  settled on yet  one way the biden administration could  enact some change without congressional  approval  is through regulation rather than  changing the tax law directly  the biden administration could use its  regulatory powers to change how the law  is interpreted  one can imagine by an administration  going in there and saying all right well  you know think on this definition for  how foreign income of u.s companies is  taxed they kind of went too far and  we're going to take some of that back  another tactic biden could take is  beefing up the irs  by asking congress for more money to  fund the agency  which could then spend more time on  enforcement and litigation  there's gonna be a lot to do a lot to  think about and and we'll just sort of  gauge these two big uncertainties which  are what the state of the pandemic and  economy look like  and what the state of the senate looks  like it's it's this odd moment where we  have  so much new clarity because we know the  election result and yet they're the  still  because life is uncertain there's still  still these big  uncertainties out there

